{"OAuthLoginView uses pipeline.signature as the OAuth state, but that value is a deterministic hash of pipeline view class names and therefore predictable, weakening CSRF protection for the OAuth callback. A random per-session state should be generated and stored in pipeline state, then validated against that stored value.": 0.7687563896179199, "GitHubInstallation compares against integration.metadata['sender']['login'] without checking the sender key exists, which can raise KeyError and 500 the flow for integrations missing this metadata (e.g., legacy rows or unexpected creation paths).": 0.9524087905883789, "OAuthLoginView does not catch exceptions from safe_urlopen or get_user_info, so transient network/HTTP failures can raise and produce a 500 instead of the intended installation-failed page. This makes the installation flow fragile and can leak internal error pages to users.": 0.9365134835243225, "OAuthLoginView does not store request.subdomain into pipeline state before redirecting to GitHub, so PipelineAdvancer cannot enforce redirecting the callback onto the correct customer-domain host. This can break the OAuth callback flow for organizations using customer domains.": 0.2477312535047531, "The new GitHub authenticated user validation is skipped if the Integration object doesn't exist yet in the database. Catching Integration.DoesNotExist early in GitHubInstallation.dispatch immediately returns pipeline.next_step(), bypassing the user check and allowing an attacker to finish installation before the webhook creates the Integration.": 0.8417835831642151}